[common]
displayName="DeepSeek Harness"
desc="The open-source, plugin-based coding agent from DeepSeek AI. It serves the DSH Web interface through the fnOS unified gateway and runs on the DSH 0.2.2-rc.1 baseline.<br /><br /><strong>Highlights</strong><br />1. Reach DSH through the NAS Web unified gateway; the service listens on 127.0.0.1 only by default.<br />2. Ships the fnOS plugin; Codex Auth and Failover Search are no longer bundled since 8.1.4 and can be installed from the Discover page or the plugin detail page (versions follow the DSH 0.2.2-rc.1 baseline and compatibility is judged by the new rules); upgrades keep credentials, model settings, sessions and workspaces.<br />3. Grants NAS directory access and remembers authorized directories (kept across upgrades and restarts, pruned automatically once a permission lapses); supports <code>/fn</code> file references, attachments, session log export and opening files in their apps. The fnOS icon above the input area opens a wide panel in place of the command palette, sized to the input box: create folders and empty files, right-click to open or rename an entry (folders open in the file manager, files in fnOS preview), and reopening the panel restores the last folder and scroll position.<br />4. The session log menu matches DSH upstream: under &quot;Download Session log&quot; you can export to your computer or to the NAS, plus a &quot;Feedback&quot; entry pointing at this repository.<br />5. Bundles the <code>fnos-cli</code> Agent Skill (with its own launcher that downloads and verifies the matching CLI version on first use); if you already installed it yourself it is skipped rather than overwritten.<br />6. Provides an interactive terminal in the sidebar that picks an available bash and injects a UTF-8 environment.<br />7. <strong>Plugin management</strong> happens in-app: installed plugins render as cards you can enable/disable, update or uninstall (with in-place confirmation); installs and updates pick the version matching the current DSH baseline, run through pnpm and stream logs live; cards flag &quot;new version X.Y.Z&quot;. The toolbox adds <strong>npm mirror management</strong> (bundled npm/yarn/cnpm/tencent/taobao/npmMirror/huawei sources, one-click switch), <strong>custom plugin indexes</strong> (add several sources and browse them on the Discover page) and <strong>fix plugin install issues</strong> (clears stale release-age exemptions, rebuilds profile dependencies and reinstalls managed plugins; the profile config is backed up into the app share's backup folder first, and the per-step log stays on the card). Third-party plugin API gateway prefixes are detected automatically; plugins use the npm registry configured in the app's <code>HOME/.npmrc</code>; after an uninstall that keeps data, reinstalling restores the reverse-proxy configuration from cache automatically.<br />8. Publishes a plugin market index: add the source on the DSH &quot;Discover&quot; page to browse and install all seven plugins, with localized names, descriptions and official icons. The Runtime page shows CPU/memory/disk/network in a four-tile layout with charts, plus this app's process tree and per-process termination.<br />9. Install, upgrade and runtime configuration stay idempotent: upgrades migrate stale prompt-variable references, and failures keep existing user data; &quot;trusted access address&quot; is validated the same way in the install, upgrade and config wizards and only accepts a host or host:port (several allowed, comma-separated), so a bad value is rejected on the spot instead of surfacing later as &quot;check Node.js dependencies&quot; after an app that will not start.<br /><br /><strong>Shortcuts</strong><br />1. <code>F5</code>, <code>Ctrl + R</code> on Windows/Linux or <code>⌘ + R</code> on macOS: refresh the DSH Web iframe only.<br />2. <code>Ctrl + ,</code> on Windows/Linux or <code>⌘ + ,</code> on macOS: open the DSH settings panel.<br />3. While picking a NAS file under <code>/fn</code>, press <code>Tab</code> to enter a subdirectory.<br />4. In the third-party plugin API URL reverse-proxy settings, press <code>Ctrl/⌘ + S</code> to save.<br /><br /><strong>Installation notes</strong><br />The package comes in x86 and ARM builds with the platform in the file name; pick the one matching your machine (app center refuses to install a package for the wrong platform). The ARM build has not been verified on real hardware by the publisher and is community-tested. The install wizard configures the port, the trusted access address and an npm registry; the listen address is always 127.0.0.1. Enter only a host or host:port for &quot;trusted access address&quot; (for example <code>192.0.2.10:5666</code>), without <code>http://</code> or a path, separated by commas, and wrap IPv6 addresses in brackets. If installation fails, check <code>/var/log/apps/fn-deepseek-harness.log</code> first, keep your existing app data, then retry."
changelog="This release turns <strong>plugin management</strong> into a complete in-app workflow and adds the <strong>Runtime</strong> and <strong>Toolbox</strong> groups. Previously plugins were a read-only list: installs failed, updates were invisible and the cause was never shown. All of that is now handled in the app.<br /><br /><strong>Plugin management</strong><br />1. <strong>Installed plugins render as cards</strong>: each card can enable/disable, update or uninstall, with in-place confirmation. dshmarket is no longer bundled and installs from the Discover page.<br />2. <strong>Install plugins in-app</strong>: the version matching the current DSH baseline is picked automatically, the install runs through pnpm and <strong>streams logs live</strong> — no more switching back to a terminal.<br />3. <strong>Update detection</strong>: cards flag &quot;new version X.Y.Z&quot;; a local version newer than the registry no longer reports &quot;up to date&quot; by mistake.<br />4. <strong>npm mirror management (nrm)</strong>: bundled npm/yarn/cnpm/tencent/taobao/npmMirror/huawei sources, one-click switch with the active one highlighted, so a failed install is no longer a guessing game about the network.<br />5. <strong>Custom plugin indexes</strong>: add several index sources and browse/install from the Discover page; entries carry localized names, descriptions and official icons.<br />6. <strong>Fix plugin install issues</strong>: one action clears stale release-age exemptions, rebuilds the profile dependencies and reinstalls the plugins this app manages. Only rebuildable artifacts are touched — credentials, sessions, workspaces and settings stay intact. The profile config is backed up into the app share&rsquo;s backup folder first, and both the outcome and the <strong>per-step log</strong> stay on the card instead of flashing past.<br /><br /><strong>Runtime and toolbox</strong><br />7. <strong>Runtime group</strong>: a four-tile layout shows CPU/memory/disk/network with ECharts curves; it also lists this app&rsquo;s process tree and can terminate individual processes.<br />8. <strong>Third-party plugin proxy</strong>: plugin API prefixes are detected and filled in automatically, with its own menu group.<br /><br /><strong>Mobile</strong><br />9. The New folder / New file / Rename dialogs fit the viewport, padding drops to 16px and titles/buttons are no longer clipped; input font size rises to 16px on touch (iOS Safari zooms the page below that) with a 40px box; buttons reach 44px and the close target grows to 44&times;44.<br />10. The file/folder panel supports a <strong>long-press menu</strong>; read-only paths (app share directories) no longer offer delete/rename; &quot;copy original path&quot; is available in every environment.<br />11. The logs page, session-log export dialog, index-source panel and settings panel are re-laid-out for narrow screens; the horizontal nav fits three and a half items per row.<br /><br /><strong>Other fixes</strong><br />12. Dialogs and overlays use DSH theme variables instead of hard-coded colors; light button disabled states follow the theme; gray fills on authorized-directory rows, the active mirror and plugin cards are gone in light mode, with borders carrying the boundaries.<br />13. The context-menu separator only appears when there is really a group above it; the uninstall tooltip now uses Tooltip and coexists with the confirmation popover.<br />14. Busy actions use <code>loading</code> only and never combine it with <code>disabled</code> (this rule is now part of the plugin UI standards).<br />15. The install wizard no longer offers the retired npmMirror source. Environments that already have it persisted switch back to the official npm registry on the next install or upgrade. Install failures now show which registry was used."

